<SddReviewLifecycle keywords="sdd, spec, semantic-review, operator-approval" type="directive" ver="2.3">
  <Mission>
    Establish approval #1 for the specs this change semantically edits. One fresh independent reviewer judges
    semantic coherence; the operator approves the reviewed artifacts. Resume uses only durable artifacts.
  </Mission>
  <BeliefState>
    {{> "axiom/process/ax-stateless-flow"}}
    {{> "axiom/process/ax-operator-language"}}
    {{> "axiom/process/ax-operator-dialogue-style"}}
    {{> "axiom/process/ax-tool-invocation"}}
    {{> "axiom/process/ax-cap-5"}}
    {{> "axiom/critic/ax-default-accept"}}
  </BeliefState>
  <Contracts>
    {{> "contract/process/artifact-approval-flow"}}
    {{> "contract/process/artifact-approval-marker"}}
  </Contracts>
  <ExecutionPlan>
    <Step id="STEP_1_FREEZE_TARGET">
      <Goal>Identify the specs whose meaning is being approved and the specs read as context.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Use actual spec paths and their current content. The approval target is exactly the specs
        whose text this change semantically edits: set approval #1 to `pending` in those specs only,
        and list those specs in the new `Reviewed set`. Add as read-only context every unedited
        parent, child, interface, infrastructure, or dependent spec needed to judge consistency;
        context specs keep their current approval marker per `ARTIFACT_APPROVAL_MARKER`, including
        specs that share an older `Reviewed set` with a target. Review actual files, with enough
        context to expose cross-spec effects. Per `AX_STATELESS_FLOW`, the target set is the whole
        review state. Run the owning structural/integrity checks first.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_2_INDEPENDENT_REVIEW">
      <Goal>Obtain one semantic judgment independent from the author.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Load `critic-protocol.directive.xml`, then dispatch one fresh reviewer with its full
        contents as the reviewer's own governing instructions, plus the exact target specs, the
        read-only context specs named as context, and
        `Polish: <on|off>` (on only when the operator explicitly asked for polish/nits this round;
        off by default). The reviewer reads but never edits. It checks requirements, negative/failure
        behavior, interfaces, dependencies, invariants, verification strategy, contradictions, and
        missing decisions. It returns `CLEAN` or at most five evidence-backed findings naming file and
        anchor. The reviewer reads only the exact target and context specs plus the operator brief supplied in
        its prompt; it does not read flow directives, examples, repository history, source code, or
        search the repository. When a target is a refinement of a previously approved spec, put that
        spec's Git diff into the prompt: blocking findings concern the changed sections and their
        direct contradictions with unchanged text, and older debt the reviewer meets comes back as a
        separate non-blocking list that the decision card shows under «На потом». It performs one semantic pass and returns the verdict without
        restating the route or narrating its internal analysis.

        The author may mentally inspect its own work before this dispatch, but that does not count as
        independent review. Do not serialize a plan into JSON, retain the reviewer, or track anything
        about prior rounds beyond the bare `AX_CAP_5` count STEP_3_RECONCILE keeps.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_3_RECONCILE">
      <Goal>Make the proposed specs match accepted review conclusions.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Per `AX_DEFAULT_ACCEPT`, classify each returned finding before acting on it: ACCEPT only an
        artifact gap backed by concrete evidence naming what breaks, for whom, and when; REJECT a
        preference, alternative design, missing local convention, or unevidenced question — uncertainty
        alone never blocks. An ACCEPTed finding whose correction keeps the agreed behaviour — a
        contradiction removed, a missing negative path of already agreed behaviour, an untestable
        wording made testable — is corrected in the owning spec at once, without a stop; the operator
        sees all such corrections once, in the STEP_4 card. An ACCEPTed finding whose resolution is
        a choice of product behaviour goes to the operator as a question: batch such findings into
        one `AskUserQuestion`, each with the smallest working option first, and edit only after the
        answer. For each REJECTed finding, list the concrete disagreement in the STEP_4 card instead
        of editing. An ACCEPTed finding
        that requires a semantic edit of a context spec moves that spec into the target set and resets
        its marker; a context spec without such a finding stays approved. Word every finding shown to
        the operator per `AX_OPERATOR_DIALOGUE_STYLE`: what is wrong and what breaks in plain words
        first, requirement / Decision Log / review-dimension tokens in parentheses after, each finding
        restated in full instead of referenced by its number. Run structural checks after edits. Any semantic edit resets the applicable approval
        markers to `pending` and therefore requires one fresh STEP_2 review of the new complete
        target. Give that reviewer the prior findings and corrected target; it verifies those fixes
        plus one contradiction sweep, then returns `CLEAN` or at most five remaining findings. This
        is a new bounded verification, not continuation of a reviewer session and not a reason to
        reload unrelated artifacts.

        Per `AX_CAP_5`, this STEP_2⇄STEP_3 review⇄reconcile cycle is bounded, not open-ended: count
        every STEP_2 dispatch across the whole review. At the fifth result — regardless of that
        result's verdict — stop dispatching and put an explicit disposition to the operator before
        another STEP_2 call or STEP_4 approval: `CLEAN` only when that fifth round made no edits;
        otherwise `CONTINUE THROUGH ROUND N` naming an explicit higher cap, or `RESTART: reason`. A
        raised cap is bounded the same way when it is in turn reached. The reviewer's verdict never
        authorizes continuation past the cap by itself.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_4_OPERATOR_APPROVAL_1">
      <Goal>Ask the operator to approve the reviewed specifications.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Present a compact decision card: exact target spec paths, key requirements and negative scenarios,
        dependencies/interfaces, verification promises, independent verdict, and accepted risks. Name
        context specs in one line as read and unchanged; the operator approves the target specs only.
        Per `AX_OPERATOR_DIALOGUE_STYLE`, the card states each item's meaning in plain words with its
        token in parentheses.
        List the corrections made from review findings and the rejected findings, one line each.
        Ask `Approve specs` or `Return for correction`. On approval, update the human-readable
        approval #1 Decision Log markers exactly as `ARTIFACT_APPROVAL_MARKER` defines. Record durable
        product/architecture decisions in the same specs; do not record workflow state outside those
        artifacts. A new session checks marker shape mechanically and uses Git evidence plus semantic
        judgment to detect staleness; it never computes a fingerprint.
      </Action>
    </Step>
  </ExecutionPlan>
</SddReviewLifecycle>
